{"entry": {"third_party_name": "Open Web Application Security Project (OWASP)", "third_party_description": "An open community dedicated to enabling organizations to develop, purchase, and maintain applications that can be trusted.", "third_party_page_ref": "https://owasp.org/", "third_party_confidence": 0.9, "distance_from_so_keywords": 1, "likely_so": "Yes", "common_so": "Yes", "third_party_controls": [{"third_party_control_seq": 1, "third_party_control_id": null, "third_party_control_desc": "Perform vulnerability scanning on a monthly basis"}], "source_context": "nsible for \nensuring adherence to the security policy which addresses logical access control procedures. \nIndividual access capabilities are removed within 48 hours by IT or data owners upon the notification of \ntermination of employment, change of responsibilities, or termination of a contract with a client that uses the \nsystem. System security access levels are reviewed quarterly by IT and data owners to ensure individual access \nrights are appropriate based on job information. \nChange Management \nSimpleLegal maintains a documented Change Management Policy to guide personnel in documenting and \nimplementing application and infrastructure changes. Change control procedures include change request and \ninitiation processes, documentation requirements, development practices, quality assurance testing requirements, \nand required approval procedures.  \nA ticketing system is utilized to document the change control procedures for changes in the application and \nimplementation of new changes"}, "reason": "This is a framework, not a company or service provider.", "type": "framework"}
{"entry": {"third_party_name": "Third-party vendor", "third_party_description": "Performs vulnerability scanning on a monthly basis using industry standard scanning technologies and a formal methodology specified by OWASP.", "third_party_page_ref": "Page 12", "third_party_confidence": 0.9, "distance_from_so_keywords": 3, "likely_so": "Yes", "common_so": "Yes", "third_party_controls": [{"third_party_control_seq": 1, "third_party_control_id": null, "third_party_control_desc": "Perform vulnerability scanning on a monthly basis"}, {"third_party_control_seq": 2, "third_party_control_id": null, "third_party_control_desc": "Use industry standard scanning technologies and a formal methodology specified by OWASP"}, {"third_party_control_seq": 3, "third_party_control_id": null, "third_party_control_desc": "Perform retests and on-demand scans as needed"}], "source_context": "nd controls. These \nmethods include the following: \n• \nNew hire training \n• \nOngoing training \n• \nPolicy and process updates \n• \nDepartmental meetings summarizing events and changes \n• \nUse of e-mail systems \nSystem Monitoring \nSimpleLegal performs system monitoring activities to continuously assess the quality of internal control over time \nand ensure that any corrective actions are completed in a timely manner. Examples of system monitoring \nprocesses in place include: \n• \nFirewalls – Firewalls protecting the internal network from the public network are implemented, configured, \nand managed by the SimpleLegal administration staff. Firewalls utilize access rules to grant or deny \naccess to internal resources.  \n• \nVulnerability Scanning – Scanning is performed by a third-party vendor on a monthly basis in accordance \nwith SimpleLegal policy. The third-party vendor uses industry standard scanning technologies and a \nformal methodology specified by Open Web Application Security Project "}, "reason": "This is a third-party vendor performing a service, not a company or cloud provider.", "type": "department"}
